    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d66">

                <pb facs="0139" n="93" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v02/pg93"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860c.01/85½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-29">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">29 January, 1918</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I think I see this
                    situation as a whole, and of course I am disposed to help in every way possible,
                    but I do not feel at liberty to pledge thirty thousand dollars a month
                    indefinitely. Would the Committee think it fair if I were to limit the pledge to
                    (say) six months, pending developments?</p>
                <p>And,—another question,—is it not likely that the portion to [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">the</hi>] British Government is to pay would in fact be
                    drawn from our Treasury, by loan? I feel obliged to think of the financial
                    burdens piling up on us.</p>
                <p>Faithfully Yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
